SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 302 | Monday, March 2, 2026

Good morning.

1) LA and Long Beach: February down 9% on January, up 3% on last year
Loaded imports at the two ports totaled 790,000 TEU. Empties out rose 14%, which is carriers repositioning boxes to Asia ahead of an anticipated spring uptick. Long Beach's executive director called it "a normal February," a phrase nobody has used about a February since 2019.

2) TPM hallway consensus: contracts settle 5-8% below last year
Shippers went in asking for flat and got better than flat. Carriers are holding the line on the surcharge structure instead, and several are pushing two-year deals with a rate-review clause. If you sign one, read the review clause twice.

3) FDA gives food traceability rule another year
Enforcement of the FSMA 204 traceability recordkeeping requirements moves to January 2028. Produce and seafood distributors get the breathing room they asked for; the software vendors who built their 2026 pipeline on the deadline do not. Expect a quiet quarter in that corner of the market.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 47 feet. The Panama Canal's maximum draft for the Neopanamax locks through the dry season, unchanged from last year and a relief after 2024.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

